# Limmer, O'Keefe & Dickinson - Emergency Care (Brady/Pearson).
# Chapter list: 15th edition (2026), from the publisher's table of contents (pearson.com).
# Chapter numbers follow the order listed there. Summaries are written for this site, not copied from the book.

def ch(n, title, text, keys):
    return (n, title, [text], keys)

C = [
    ch(1, "Introduction to Emergency Medical Services", "EMS is a system of care from dispatch to hospital. Provider levels are EMR, EMT, AEMT, and Paramedic. The medical director oversees care through online (direct) and offline (protocols, standing orders) medical direction. EMTs take part in quality improvement and must keep certification current.", ["EMR, EMT, AEMT, Paramedic", "Medical director", "Online vs. offline medical direction", "Quality improvement"]),
    ch(2, "Well-Being of the EMT", "EMT safety comes first: scene safety, standard precautions and PPE, and immunizations. Stress management helps prevent burnout. Critical incident stress can follow difficult calls. Grief stages are denial, anger, bargaining, depression, and acceptance.", ["Scene safety first", "Standard precautions", "Manage stress", "Grief stages"]),
    ch(3, "Lifting and Moving Patients", "Use good body mechanics: lift with the legs, keep the back straight and the load close, and use a power grip and power lift. Emergency moves (like the drag) are used for immediate danger; urgent moves for life threats; non-urgent moves otherwise. Equipment includes stretchers, stair chairs, and scoop stretchers.", ["Lift with legs", "Keep load close", "Emergency moves for danger", "Know your equipment"]),
    ch(4, "Medical, Legal, and Ethical Issues", "Scope of practice defines what an EMT may do. Consent can be expressed, implied (unresponsive patients), or involuntary. Competent adults may refuse care after being informed of risks. Negligence requires duty, breach, damages, and causation. Abandonment, assault, battery, and confidentiality (HIPAA) are key issues, as are DNR orders and crime scenes.", ["Expressed vs. implied consent", "Refusal needs informed patient", "Four elements of negligence", "Don't abandon patients"]),
    ch(5, "Medical Terminology", "Medical terms are built from prefixes, roots, and suffixes. Anatomic terms include anterior/posterior, medial/lateral, proximal/distal, superior/inferior, and positions such as supine, prone, Fowler's, and recovery position. Use only approved abbreviations.", ["Prefix, root, suffix", "Supine = face up; prone = face down", "Fowler's = sitting up", "Proximal vs. distal"]),
    ch(6, "Anatomy and Physiology", "EMTs study the body's systems: respiratory, cardiovascular, nervous, musculoskeletal, endocrine, digestive, integumentary, and more. Key facts include the heart's chambers and blood flow, arteries carrying blood away from the heart, and the brain and spinal cord forming the central nervous system.", ["Body systems", "Arteries carry blood away", "CNS = brain + spinal cord", "Blood flow through the heart"]),
    ch(7, "Principles of Pathophysiology", "Cells need oxygen and glucose to make energy. Perfusion is delivery of oxygenated blood to tissues; hypoperfusion is shock. Problems with the airway, breathing, heart, blood volume, or vessels cause hypoperfusion. Compensation includes increased heart and breathing rates.", ["Perfusion = oxygen delivery", "Hypoperfusion = shock", "Body compensates", "Cells need O2 and glucose"]),
    ch(8, "Life Span Development", "Vital signs and behaviors change with age. Infants have faster heart and breathing rates; toddlers fear separation; adolescents value privacy; older adults have decreased organ function and may have atypical presentations.", ["Infants: faster vitals", "Toddlers: separation anxiety", "Teens value privacy", "Older adults: atypical presentations"]),
    ch(9, "Airway Management", "Open the airway with the head-tilt, chin-lift or, for suspected spine injury, the jaw-thrust. Suction for no more than 15 seconds in adults. Airway adjuncts include oropharyngeal airways (only for patients without a gag reflex) and nasopharyngeal airways.", ["Jaw-thrust for spine injury", "Suction ≤ 15 sec adults", "OPA only if no gag reflex", "NPA for patients with gag reflex"]),
    ch(10, "Respiration and Artificial Ventilation", "Adequate breathing has normal rate, depth, and effort. Inadequate breathing needs ventilation with a bag-valve mask (BVM), ideally by two rescuers. Oxygen devices include the nonrebreather mask (10–15 L/min) and nasal cannula (1–6 L/min). CPAP helps conscious patients in respiratory distress.", ["BVM for inadequate breathing", "Nonrebreather 10–15 L/min", "Nasal cannula 1–6 L/min", "CPAP for respiratory distress"]),
    ch(11, "Scene Size-Up", "Scene size-up includes scene safety, standard precautions, determining the mechanism of injury or nature of illness, number of patients, and need for additional resources.", ["Scene safety", "Mechanism of injury", "Number of patients", "Call for resources"]),
    ch(12, "Primary Assessment", "The primary assessment finds and treats life threats: general impression, mental status (AVPU), airway, breathing, circulation (pulse, bleeding, skin), and patient priority. Severe bleeding may be controlled first (XABC).", ["AVPU", "Treat life threats immediately", "Control severe bleeding", "Determine priority"]),
    ch(13, "Vital Signs and Monitoring Devices", "Vital signs include breathing, pulse, skin (color, temperature, condition), pupils, blood pressure, and SpO2. Normal adult pulse is 60–100, respirations 12–20. Pulse oximetry, blood glucose meters, and capnography assist assessment.", ["Pulse 60–100", "Respirations 12–20", "Skin color, temp, condition", "SpO2 and glucose"]),
    ch(14, "Principles of Assessment", "Assessment combines history, exam, and clinical reasoning. Use OPQRST for pain (onset, provocation, quality, region/radiation, severity, time) and SAMPLE history (signs/symptoms, allergies, medications, past history, last oral intake, events).", ["OPQRST", "SAMPLE history", "Clinical reasoning", "Differential thinking"]),
    ch(15, "Secondary Assessment", "The secondary assessment is a detailed exam—rapid trauma or focused medical exam. DCAP-BTLS looks for deformities, contusions, abrasions, punctures/penetrations, burns, tenderness, lacerations, and swelling.", ["DCAP-BTLS", "Rapid trauma assessment", "Focused medical exam", "Head-to-toe"]),
    ch(16, "Reassessment", "Reassess unstable patients every 5 minutes and stable patients every 15 minutes, repeating the primary assessment, vital signs, and checking interventions.", ["Unstable: every 5 min", "Stable: every 15 min", "Check interventions", "Trend vital signs"]),
    ch(17, "Communication and Documentation", "Communicate clearly by radio with dispatch and hospitals. The prehospital care report (PCR) is a legal document. Document objectively and accurately, including refusals. Therapeutic communication builds trust with patients.", ["PCR is a legal document", "Document refusals", "Clear radio reports", "Objective documentation"]),
    ch(18, "General Pharmacology", "EMTs may give or assist with medications such as oxygen, oral glucose, aspirin, activated charcoal (per protocol), epinephrine auto-injectors, nitroglycerin, inhalers, and naloxone. Follow the rights of medication administration: right patient, drug, dose, route, time, and documentation.", ["Rights of medication administration", "Aspirin for chest pain", "Naloxone for opioid overdose", "Assist with patient's nitro and inhaler"]),
    ch(19, "Respiratory Emergencies", "Respiratory emergencies include asthma, COPD, pneumonia, pulmonary embolism, and pulmonary edema. Signs of distress include tripod position, accessory muscle use, and cyanosis. Give oxygen, assist with inhalers, and use CPAP when indicated.", ["Tripod position", "Accessory muscle use", "Assist with inhaler", "CPAP when indicated"]),
    ch(20, "Cardiac Emergencies", "Acute coronary syndrome causes chest pain or discomfort that may radiate. Treatment includes oxygen if hypoxic, aspirin, and nitroglycerin (not if systolic BP under 100 or recent erectile dysfunction drug use). Heart failure causes dyspnea and edema.", ["Aspirin for ACS", "Nitro contraindications", "Women and diabetics may have atypical symptoms", "Transport quickly"]),
    ch(21, "Resuscitation", "Cardiac arrest management: high-quality CPR (100–120/min, at least 2 inches deep in adults), minimal interruptions, and early AED use. The chain of survival links recognition, CPR, defibrillation, advanced care, post-arrest care, and recovery.", ["CPR 100–120/min", "Early AED", "Minimize interruptions", "Chain of survival"]),
    ch(22, "Diabetic Emergencies and Altered Mental Status", "Hypoglycemia comes on fast with cool, clammy skin and confusion; give oral glucose if the patient can swallow. Hyperglycemia develops slowly with thirst, urination, and fruity breath (DKA). Other causes of altered mental status include stroke (use a stroke scale), seizures, and syncope.", ["Oral glucose if able to swallow", "Hypoglycemia: rapid onset", "DKA: fruity breath", "Stroke scale"]),
    ch(23, "Allergic Reactions", "Anaphylaxis is a severe allergic reaction with airway swelling, wheezing, hives, and low blood pressure. Treat with epinephrine (auto-injector in the lateral thigh), oxygen, and rapid transport.", ["Anaphylaxis = airway + shock", "Epinephrine auto-injector", "Lateral thigh", "Rapid transport"]),
    ch(24, "Infectious Diseases and Sepsis", "Protect yourself with standard precautions and PPE. Diseases include hepatitis, HIV, TB (N95 respirator), and influenza. Sepsis is a life-threatening response to infection; signs include fever or low temperature, tachycardia, rapid breathing, and altered mental status.", ["Standard precautions", "N95 for TB", "Sepsis recognition", "Hand hygiene"]),
    ch(25, "Poisoning and Overdose Emergencies", "Poisons enter by ingestion, inhalation, injection, or absorption. Contact poison control and medical direction. Opioid overdose causes respiratory depression and pinpoint pupils; give naloxone and ventilate. Remove patients from toxic atmospheres safely.", ["Four routes of poisoning", "Opioids: pinpoint pupils", "Naloxone", "Contact poison control"]),
    ch(26, "Abdominal Emergencies", "Abdominal pain can be visceral (dull, poorly localized), parietal (sharp, localized), or referred. Causes include appendicitis, bowel obstruction, and aortic aneurysm. Position comfortably, give nothing by mouth, and treat for shock.", ["Visceral vs. parietal pain", "Referred pain", "Nothing by mouth", "AAA can be deadly"]),
    ch(27, "Behavioral and Psychiatric Emergencies and Suicide", "Behavioral emergencies require scene safety, calm communication, and assessment for medical causes (hypoglycemia, hypoxia). Take suicide threats seriously. Restraint follows protocol and law; positional asphyxia must be prevented.", ["Ensure safety", "Rule out medical causes", "Take suicide seriously", "Restraint per protocol"]),
    ch(28, "Hematologic and Renal Emergencies", "Hematologic disorders include sickle cell disease (painful crises) and clotting disorders. Renal emergencies include kidney failure and dialysis complications. Don't take blood pressure in the arm with a dialysis fistula.", ["Sickle cell crisis", "Clotting disorders", "Dialysis complications", "Avoid BP on fistula arm"]),
    ch(29, "Bleeding and Shock", "Control external bleeding with direct pressure, then a tourniquet for life-threatening limb bleeding, or wound packing for junctional wounds. Shock types include hypovolemic, cardiogenic, distributive, and obstructive. Signs include restlessness, tachycardia, and pale, cool skin; low blood pressure is a late sign.", ["Direct pressure, then tourniquet", "Hypotension is a late sign", "Types of shock", "Keep patient warm"]),
    ch(30, "Soft-Tissue Trauma", "Closed injuries include contusions and hematomas; open injuries include abrasions, lacerations, avulsions, punctures, and amputations. Cover open chest wounds with an occlusive dressing. Stabilize impaled objects in place. Burns are classified by depth and estimated with the rule of nines.", ["Stabilize impaled objects", "Occlusive dressing for chest wounds", "Rule of nines", "Save amputated parts cool and dry"]),
    ch(31, "Chest and Abdominal Trauma", "Chest injuries include pneumothorax, tension pneumothorax, hemothorax, flail chest, and cardiac tamponade. Abdominal injuries include evisceration (cover with moist, sterile dressing; don't push organs back) and internal bleeding.", ["Tension pneumothorax", "Flail chest: paradoxical motion", "Evisceration: moist dressing", "Don't replace organs"]),
    ch(32, "Musculoskeletal Trauma", "Injuries include fractures, dislocations, sprains, and strains. Splint injuries in the position found, immobilizing the joints above and below, and check pulse, motor, and sensation before and after. Traction splints are for midshaft femur fractures.", ["Check PMS before and after", "Immobilize joints above and below", "Traction splint for femur", "Splint in position found"]),
    ch(33, "Trauma to the Head, Neck, and Spine", "Head injuries include concussion and bleeding in the skull; signs of rising pressure include altered mental status and Cushing's triad (high BP, slow pulse, irregular breathing). Use spinal motion restriction for suspected spine injury. Monitor GCS.", ["Cushing's triad", "Spinal motion restriction", "GCS", "Altered mental status"]),
    ch(34, "Multisystem Trauma", "Multisystem trauma patients need rapid assessment, life-threat treatment, and transport to a trauma center. Use the field triage guidelines. Minimize scene time (the 'golden period').", ["Trauma center", "Field triage guidelines", "Limit scene time", "Treat life threats first"]),
    ch(35, "Environmental Emergencies", "Heat emergencies include heat cramps, heat exhaustion, and heat stroke (hot skin, altered mental status; cool rapidly). Cold emergencies include hypothermia and frostbite. Others: drowning, bites and stings, diving and altitude injuries, and lightning.", ["Heat stroke: cool rapidly", "Hypothermia: handle gently", "Frostbite", "Drowning"]),
    ch(36, "Obstetric and Gynecologic Emergencies", "Labor has three stages. Assist delivery by supporting the head, checking for the cord around the neck, and drying and warming the newborn. Assess newborns with the APGAR score. Complications include breech birth, prolapsed cord (relieve pressure on the cord), eclampsia, and postpartum bleeding.", ["Three stages of labor", "APGAR", "Prolapsed cord: relieve pressure", "Keep newborn warm"]),
    ch(37, "Emergencies for Patients with Special Challenges", "Patients with special challenges include those with disabilities, technology dependence (home ventilators, tracheostomies, feeding tubes, VADs), terminal illness, and older adults. Caregivers are a valuable resource. Watch for abuse and neglect.", ["Caregivers are experts", "Home medical devices", "Watch for abuse", "Respect DNR wishes"]),
    ch(38, "EMS Operations", "Phases of an EMS call include preparation, dispatch, en route, arrival, transfer, and post-run. Drive with due regard; lights and sirens don't guarantee right of way. Air medical transport may be used; landing zones need to be secure.", ["Phases of a call", "Due regard driving", "Air medical landing zones", "Clean and restock"]),
    ch(39, "Hazardous Materials, Multiple-Casualty Incidents, and Incident Management", "Hazmat scenes have hot, warm, and cold zones; EMTs work in the cold zone. The Emergency Response Guidebook helps identify materials. The Incident Command System organizes response. Triage systems like START sort patients by priority.", ["Hot, warm, cold zones", "ERG guidebook", "Incident Command System", "START triage"]),
    ch(40, "Highway Safety and Vehicle Extrication", "Highway safety includes high-visibility vests, positioning the ambulance to protect the scene, and traffic control. Extrication steps: size-up, stabilize the vehicle, gain access, disentangle, and remove the patient.", ["Wear high-visibility vests", "Stabilize the vehicle", "Gain access", "Protect the patient"]),
    ch(41, "EMS Response to Terrorism", "Terrorism may involve chemical, biological, radiological, nuclear, or explosive (CBRNE) weapons. Recognize indicators, avoid secondary devices, protect yourself with time, distance, and shielding, and follow incident command. Nerve agents cause SLUDGEM signs.", ["CBRNE", "Secondary devices", "Time, distance, shielding", "Nerve agents: SLUDGEM"]),
]

